[section:fresnel Fresnel Integrals]

[h4 Synopsis]

    #include <boost/math/special_functions/fresnel.hpp>

    namespace boost { namespace math {

    template <class T>
    ``__sf_result`` fresnel_c(T x);
    template <class T, class ``__Policy``>
    ``__sf_result`` fresnel_c(T x, const ``__Policy``&);

    template <class T>
    ``__sf_result`` fresnel_s(T x);
    template <class T, class ``__Policy``>
    ``__sf_result`` fresnel_s(T x, const ``__Policy``&);

    // C(x) + i S(x):
    template <class T>
    ``['complex-type]`` fresnel_cis(T x);
    template <class T, class ``__Policy``>
    ``['complex-type]`` fresnel_cis(T x, const ``__Policy``&);

    }} // namespaces

[h4 Description]

The Fresnel integrals are

[expression C(x) = [int][sub 0][super x] cos([pi]t[super 2]/2) dt,  S(x) = [int][sub 0][super x] sin([pi]t[super 2]/2) dt]

([@https://dlmf.nist.gov/7.2.iii DLMF 7.2.7, 7.2.8]), the normalization of Abramowitz and Stegun, Mathematica, SciPy and
mpmath, in which both tend to 1/2 as /x/ [rarr] [infin]. The curve (C(x), S(x)) is the Euler (Cornu) spiral.
`fresnel_cis` returns C(x) + i S(x) = [int][sub 0][super x] cis([pi]t[super 2]/2) dt, cis = cos + i sin, for the cost of one of
them: `std::complex<__sf_result>` for float, double and long double, and Boost.Multiprecision's matching complex type
otherwise, whose header must then be included: `cpp_complex_50` for `cpp_bin_float_50`, from
`<boost/multiprecision/cpp_complex.hpp>` (likewise `<boost/multiprecision/complex128.hpp>`, `<boost/multiprecision/mpc.hpp>`).

    double c = boost::math::fresnel_c(1.0);                // 0.779893400376822829...
    std::complex<double> z = boost::math::fresnel_cis(1.0); // (0.779893400376822829..., 0.438259147390354766...)

[h4 Domain]

All real /x/. Both functions are odd, and C([plusminus][infin]) = S([plusminus][infin]) = [plusminus]1/2 exactly. A NaN
argument is a __domain_error.

[h4 Accuracy]

Relative errors in units of [epsilon], against mpmath at 80 digits at the exact arguments, 20 000 points uniform on
\[0, 12\] and log-uniform on \[10[super -6], 10[super 9]\] (5 000 points for `cpp_bin_float_50`). By default float is evaluated in double;
the second float row is evaluated in float (`promote_float<false>`).

[table
[[Type] [Series, [pi]x[super 2]/2 [le] p/10] [Continued fraction] [Asymptotic, x > [epsilon][super -1/2]]]
[[float] [0.50] [0.50] [0.08]]
[[float, in float] [0.50] [0.75] [0.50]]
[[double] [0.50] [0.69] [0.50]]
[[cpp_bin_float_50] [0.49] [0.64] [0.51]]
]

(p is the number of bits of the type; the series covers x [le] 1.84 in double.) That is correctly rounded or nearly so
everywhere; the largest errors, just above the switch to the continued fraction, are below one ulp.

[h4 Implementation]

With [theta] = [pi]x[super 2]/2 and /p/ bits of precision:

* For [theta] [le] p/10, the Maclaurin series of C + iS = x [sum][sub k] (i[theta])[super k] / (k! (2k + 1)), by Horner's rule in
[theta][super 2] in double-word arithmetic. Its terms add up to about e[super [theta]] / (2[theta]) times the result, and
[theta] itself must be known to more than working precision, since C' = cos([theta]) amplifies its error by x[super 2]/2; so
x[super 2] is formed exactly and [pi] is carried as hi + sin(hi).
* Otherwise C = 1/2 + f sin [theta] - g cos [theta] and S = 1/2 - f cos [theta] - g sin [theta]
([@https://dlmf.nist.gov/7.5 DLMF 7.5.3, 7.5.4]). The auxiliary functions are smooth: g + i f = x / (1 - 2i[theta] - 1[sdot]2 / (5 - 2i[theta] - 3[sdot]4 / (9 - 2i[theta] - ...))),
the even part of Laplace's continued fraction for erfc((1 - i)[radic][pi] x / 2) ([@https://dlmf.nist.gov/7.9.E2 DLMF 7.9.2]),
evaluated backwards from a depth of max(0.11 p[super 2]/[theta], p / (1.6 log[sub 2] [theta])) + 2 terms (measured) and
finished in double-word arithmetic. For x > [epsilon][super -1/2], f = 1/([pi]x) and g = 1/([pi][super 2]x[super 3]).
* The phase is exact: x[super 2]/2 = h + l with h reduced mod 2 exactly, sin and cos of [pi]h from [link math_toolkit.powers.sin_pi sin_pi] and [link math_toolkit.powers.cos_pi cos_pi],
and l added by the angle-addition formulas; for x [ge] 2/[epsilon], x is an even integer and the phase is 0.
* Of `fresnel_c` and `fresnel_s`, only the result asked for is narrowed to the result type, so that the other cannot raise
an underflow or overflow error.

For types whose precision is set at run time, such as `mpfr_float`, everything that depends on the precision is
recomputed at the current precision on each call.

[h4 References]

* NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, [@https://dlmf.nist.gov/7 Chapter 7].
* W. J. Cody, ['Chebyshev approximations for the Fresnel integrals], Math. Comp. 22 (1968) 450[ndash]453, gives
rational approximations of the same auxiliary functions for float and double.

[endsect] [/section:fresnel Fresnel Integrals]
